Array & Pointers
Array Name = Hidden Pointer

Every array needs one hidden pointer. The array name it should always point to the first element.

cint a[5];   // 'a' is a hidden pointer to a[0] (i.e., &a[0])
a     → &a[0] → 500
a+1   → &a[1] → 502
a+2   → &a[2] → 504
*(a)  → a[0]  (value)
*(a+1)→ a[1]
*(a+i)→ a[i]    ← general formula
Array Element Access — Equivalence
a[i]  ==  *(a+i)      both are same ✓
i[a]  ==  *(i+a)      both are same ✓ (unusual but valid!)
Justification: a[i] → pointer expression → converted into equivalent: *(a+i)

Array name is always a constant — cannot change it. It is a constant pointer expression, not a variable.

Applied with pointer expression:
* pointer expression except when it is applied with sizeof and address operators.
If we want to apply these operations we need to first dereference to a specific type.

Accessing Array Elements
cvoid main() {
    int *p;
    int a[3] = {34, 14, 84, 34};    // size 3 but 4 values → CE
}
c// Ex 1: Array with pointer
void main() {
    int *p;
    int a[3] = {34, 14, 84};    // a[0]=34, a[1]=14, a[2]=84
    p = &a[0];                   // or: p = a;

    // Accessing:
    // p[0] == *(p+0) == a[0] == 34
    // p[1] == *(p+1) == a[1] == 14
    // p[2] == *(p+2) == a[2] == 84
}

Array Traversal with Pointer
c// Ex 2
void main() {
    int a[5] = {10, 80, 30, 40, 50};
    int *p;
    p = &a[0];    // p = a;

    ++*p;         // a[0] becomes 11
    ++p;          // p moves to a[1]
    printf("%d %d %d %d\n", *p[-1], p[0], p[1], *p+1);
    // Output: 11  80  30  81
}

Array with Traversal
c// Ex 3:
void main() {
    int a[5] = {10, 80, 30, 40, 50};
    int *p;
    p = &a[0];

    ++*p;      // a[0] = 11
    ++p;       // p → a[1]
    printf("%d %d\n", p[0], *p);     // 80  80
}

